1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,088
July first, twenty twenty-five. Río Hurtado, Chile.

2
00:00:04,088 --> 00:00:14,282
A telescope built to catch killer asteroids flags something else: light moving too fast to be one of ours.

3
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,211
Astronomers confirm it within days.

4
00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:23,370
Only the third interstellar object ever detected, after one in twenty seventeen and one in twenty nineteen.

5
00:00:23,370 --> 00:00:32,066
Its orbit is the most extreme of the three, tilted almost sideways, moving in the opposite direction of everything else in the solar system.

6
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:41,992
In the first days after discovery, early brightness estimates suggested it might be up to twenty kilometers wide, the largest interstellar object ever seen.

7
00:00:41,992 --> 00:00:48,586
Weeks later, once the Hubble Space Telescope resolved it directly, that number collapsed to somewhere under six kilometers.

8
00:00:49,733 --> 00:00:56,012
A Harvard astrophysicist takes one look at the numbers and says, in public, that it might not be a comet at all.

9
00:00:56,012 --> 00:00:57,439
It might be a probe.

10
00:00:58,566 --> 00:01:02,772
Four months later, NASA holds a press conference to answer him directly.

11
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:07,699
"This object is a comet," says NASA associate administrator Amit Kshatriya.

12
00:01:07,699 --> 00:01:13,226
"It looks and behaves like a comet, and all evidence points towards it being a comet."

13
00:01:14,366 --> 00:01:21,183
That argument, one scientist convinced we might not be alone, an entire agency convinced we are, is not new.

14
00:01:21,183 --> 00:01:26,565
It is seventy-five years old, and it started, almost by accident, at a lunch table.

15
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:29,189
You're watching mindwired.

16
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:49,010
This is the real story of the Fermi Paradox: the offhand question that became a seventy-five-year argument, the real scientists who tried to answer it, and the two live, unresolved cases, one from twenty twenty-five, one still running in twenty twenty-six, that show exactly how close, and how far, we actually are.

17
00:01:50,133 --> 00:01:52,902
Chapter one. The lunch nobody wrote down.

18
00:01:54,033 --> 00:01:57,053
Summer, nineteen fifty. Los Alamos, New Mexico.

19
00:01:57,053 --> 00:02:06,546
At a lunch table inside Fuller Lodge, physicist Enrico Fermi sits down with three colleagues: Emil Konopinski, Edward Teller, and Herbert York.

20
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:23,392
On the walk over, the four men had been talking about a New Yorker cartoon published a few months earlier: a flying saucer, and little green men carrying off the neighborhood's trash cans, poking fun at a real, minor nineteen fifty news story about missing garbage cans in New York City.

21
00:02:24,533 --> 00:02:30,699
Then, with no warning, Fermi asked a question that had nothing to do with anything they'd been discussing.

22
00:02:30,699 --> 00:02:35,495
Konopinski remembered it as, "But where is everybody?" Teller remembered the same four words.

23
00:02:35,495 --> 00:02:39,606
York remembered it slightly differently: "Don't you ever wonder where everybody is?"

24
00:02:40,733 --> 00:02:44,075
Everyone at the table understood immediately he meant extraterrestrials.

25
00:02:44,075 --> 00:02:57,817
Fermi followed up with his own back-of-the-envelope math, the odds of other earth-like planets, the odds of life, the odds of intelligence, and concluded that, statistically, we should have been visited long ago, and many times over.

26
00:02:58,966 --> 00:03:05,990
Here's the honest part almost nobody mentions: the three men who were actually there never agreed on when it happened.

27
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:09,854
York said nineteen fifty-one. Teller, separately, said probably before nineteen fifty.

28
00:03:09,854 --> 00:03:21,092
A Los Alamos historian spent decades trying to pin the date down and settled on nineteen fifty, based on how fresh a cartoon still felt in one man's memory, thirty-four years later.

29
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:25,379
Fuller Lodge is still standing today, in downtown Los Alamos.

30
00:03:25,379 --> 00:03:29,469
The actual room where this happened is a museum you can walk into.

31
00:03:30,599 --> 00:03:33,760
Chapter two. The name took twenty-seven years to attach.

32
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:38,626
Fermi never published his question. He never gave it a name.

33
00:03:38,626 --> 00:03:45,741
The idea just sat there, unrecorded, until a young astronomer named Carl Sagan mentioned it in a footnote thirteen years later.

34
00:03:46,866 --> 00:03:53,443
In nineteen seventy-five, astronomer Michael Hart published the first rigorous version of the argument in a British astronomy journal.

35
00:03:53,443 --> 00:03:57,942
His paper never once mentions Fermi's name. It never uses the word paradox.

36
00:03:59,066 --> 00:04:01,840
Hart's argument didn't stop in nineteen seventy-five.

37
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:10,952
In nineteen eighty, physicist Frank Tipler extended it into what's now called the Hart-Tipler conjecture, an even harder-line version of the same case.

38
00:04:12,099 --> 00:04:21,504
That same year as Hart's paper, engineer David Viewing published a separate paper that finally called it a paradox, and credited Fermi by name for asking the original question.

39
00:04:22,633 --> 00:04:39,795
The exact phrase "Fermi's Paradox" didn't appear in print until nineteen seventy-seven, coined by physicist David Stephenson, who later admitted he only put quotation marks around it as, in his words, an apology for using a short, convenient label for a much messier argument.

40
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:43,962
The man everyone credits never wrote the paradox down.

41
00:04:43,962 --> 00:04:49,684
Three other men, spread across two years, built the argument and gave it his name, without him.

42
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:54,960
Chapter three. The same argument got a formula eleven years later.

43
00:04:56,099 --> 00:05:06,326
A year before that, Frank Drake had already run the very first real search for alien radio signals, Project Ozma, pointed at two nearby stars, spring of nineteen sixty.

44
00:05:06,326 --> 00:05:10,205
It found nothing. But it proved the search itself was possible.

45
00:05:11,333 --> 00:05:14,203
November first, nineteen sixty-one. Green Bank, West Virginia.

46
00:05:14,203 --> 00:05:22,095
Drake gathers ten scientists for a meeting that would define how the search for alien life gets talked about, to this day.

47
00:05:23,233 --> 00:05:34,671
On the first morning, Drake writes seven variables on a chalkboard: the rate stars form, the fraction with planets, the fraction that could support life, and so on, multiplied together into a single number.

48
00:05:34,671 --> 00:05:37,026
It becomes known as the Drake Equation.

49
00:05:38,166 --> 00:05:43,939
One variable in that equation is called L, how long a civilization survives before it goes silent.

50
00:05:43,939 --> 00:05:47,675
Drake built it that way on purpose. It was nineteen sixty-one.

51
00:05:47,675 --> 00:05:50,731
The Berlin Wall had gone up two months earlier.

52
00:05:50,731 --> 00:05:55,485
Every scientist at that table was living through the real possibility of nuclear war.

53
00:05:56,633 --> 00:06:08,942
A radio astronomer named Sebastian von Hoerner had already put a number on that fear, sixty percent, he estimated, was the chance any civilization wipes itself out with nuclear war once it reaches that stage.

54
00:06:08,942 --> 00:06:15,624
Soviet astronomer Iosif Shklovskii later grew so convinced of it that he renounced the search for alien signals entirely.

55
00:06:16,766 --> 00:06:22,956
In nineteen sixty-six, Shklovskii and Carl Sagan co-wrote an entire book built around that same uncertain lifespan.

56
00:06:22,956 --> 00:06:28,417
Sagan brought the same warning to a mass television audience in nineteen eighty, on Cosmos.

57
00:06:29,566 --> 00:06:33,537
Chapter four. We finally have real numbers to plug into that formula.

58
00:06:34,666 --> 00:06:37,061
For years, the working number was tiny.

59
00:06:37,061 --> 00:06:45,272
Of NASA's Kepler telescope's first thousand confirmed planets, only about a dozen were even close to Earth's size and sitting in the right zone.

60
00:06:46,399 --> 00:06:53,395
For the first four decades after that chalkboard, nobody could fill in Drake's equation with anything but guesses.

61
00:06:53,395 --> 00:06:56,893
There were zero confirmed planets outside our solar system.

62
00:06:56,893 --> 00:06:59,225
Then, in nineteen ninety-five, that changed.

63
00:07:00,366 --> 00:07:07,915
Thirty years later, NASA has confirmed more than six thousand exoplanets, with billions more believed to exist across the galaxy.

64
00:07:09,033 --> 00:07:23,061
Narrow that down to planets actually sitting in the habitable zone, rocky, roughly Earth-sized, the right distance from their star, and a twenty twenty-six catalogue from Cornell's Carl Sagan Institute finds exactly forty-five real candidates.

65
00:07:24,199 --> 00:07:26,717
Even that number is a moving target.

66
00:07:26,717 --> 00:07:35,710
Depending on the study, the fraction of sun-like stars with a truly earth-like planet ranges anywhere from one percent to as high as forty percent.

67
00:07:35,710 --> 00:07:38,228
We've never actually finished counting. We've extrapolated.

68
00:07:39,366 --> 00:07:46,890
NASA's newer TESS telescope isn't overturning that picture, it's sharpening it, tightening the uncertainty around numbers Kepler could only estimate.

69
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:52,532
This is a number actively being worked on, right now, not one anybody's finished with.

70
00:07:53,666 --> 00:07:57,793
Chapter five. Is the worst part behind us, or still ahead?

71
00:07:58,933 --> 00:08:06,090
In nineteen ninety-six, an economist, not an astronomer, named Robin Hanson wrote an essay that reframed the entire question.

72
00:08:06,090 --> 00:08:17,767
He called it the Great Filter: somewhere on the path from dead matter to a galaxy-spanning civilization, at least one step is so improbable that almost nothing ever makes it through.

73
00:08:18,899 --> 00:08:29,646
His own words: "The easier it was for life to evolve to our stage, the bleaker our future chances probably are." If most of the filter is already behind us, our future is wide open.

74
00:08:29,646 --> 00:08:35,173
If it's still ahead, something is coming that we haven't survived yet, in any case we know of.

75
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:55,369
Philosopher Nick Bostrom, then at Oxford's Future of Humanity Institute, took the ahead-of-us version seriously enough to publish a two thousand eight essay literally titled "Why I Hope the Search for Extraterrestrial Life Finds Nothing." Among his candidates for a filter still ahead of humanity: superintelligent artificial intelligence with destructive goals.

76
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:59,819
Not every scientist agrees a filter is even needed.

77
00:08:59,819 --> 00:09:14,942
In twenty eighteen, three Oxford researchers argued the paradox might simply dissolve once you model real statistical uncertainty into Drake's equation, raising the odds that we are simply, quietly, alone in this galaxy to as high as ninety-nine point six percent.

78
00:09:16,066 --> 00:09:29,099
Toby Ord, also of Oxford, later estimated our own generation faces roughly a one-in-six chance of civilization-ending catastrophe this century, nuclear war, engineered pandemics, and unaligned artificial intelligence among the largest contributors.

79
00:09:29,099 --> 00:09:36,023
If a filter really is ahead of every civilization, we may be standing inside it right now.

80
00:09:37,166 --> 00:09:40,797
Chapter six. Maybe the rare part isn't life. It's us.

81
00:09:41,933 --> 00:09:56,241
Ward and Brownlee's collaborators went even further, proposing a Galactic Habitable Zone, the idea that only certain regions of the Milky Way, at the right distance from the crowded, radiation-heavy galactic center, can support complex life at all.

82
00:09:56,241 --> 00:10:08,291
One of that paper's own co-authors later became linked to intelligent-design advocacy in a separate, far more controversial book, a reminder to judge each paper on its own record, not by association.

83
00:10:09,433 --> 00:10:16,550
In the year two thousand, geologist Peter Ward and astronomer Donald Brownlee proposed a different answer entirely.

84
00:10:16,550 --> 00:10:19,900
Simple microbial life, they argued, might be everywhere.

85
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:24,924
Complex life, anything bigger than a microbe, might be almost impossibly rare.

86
00:10:26,066 --> 00:10:40,512
Their case rests on a list of coincidences: a moon just large enough to stabilize our planet's tilt, plate tectonics that recycle carbon and keep the climate survivable for billions of years, a magnetic field shielding the atmosphere from the sun's radiation.

87
00:10:41,633 --> 00:10:47,239
They also argued Jupiter acts as a gravitational shield, sweeping up comets before they reach us.

88
00:10:47,239 --> 00:10:57,750
A later three-part study directly challenged that claim, finding a Jupiter-sized planet doesn't clearly reduce the danger at all, for some kinds of impacts, it can even make things worse.

89
00:10:58,900 --> 00:11:01,689
Other scientists pushed back hard on the whole idea.

90
00:11:01,689 --> 00:11:10,368
One reviewer called it restrictive and unimaginative, a kind of circular reasoning that just describes the one planet we happen to live on, and calls it a rule.

91
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:16,176
Chapter seven. The theory that says silence might be the only rational choice.

92
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:27,346
In two thousand eight, Chinese novelist Liu Cixin, not a scientist, a novelist, published a science-fiction idea that would eventually leak into real scientific debate.

93
00:11:27,346 --> 00:11:35,384
He called it the dark forest: every civilization is a silent, armed hunter, unable to ever verify another civilization's intentions.

94
00:11:35,384 --> 00:11:43,422
So the only rational move is total silence, and the only rational response to being noticed is to strike first.

95
00:11:44,566 --> 00:11:52,271
The idea itself is older than the novel, astronomer David Brin raised a nearly identical "deadly probes" concern back in nineteen eighty-three.

96
00:11:52,271 --> 00:12:03,479
And it's the exact logic Avi Loeb reached for, in twenty twenty-five, when he floated the possibility that the object from our cold open could be reconnaissance, not just rock and ice.

97
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:06,679
But even Loeb doesn't claim certainty.

98
00:12:06,679 --> 00:12:11,532
In his own words, this object is most likely a comet of natural origin.

99
00:12:11,532 --> 00:12:17,426
He rates his own alien-technology hypothesis a four out of ten, on a scale he invented himself.

100
00:12:18,566 --> 00:12:27,140
Penn State astronomer Jason Wright, who runs his own search for alien technology for a living, published a point-by-point rebuttal of every claimed anomaly.

101
00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:31,426
Two separate radio observatories listened to the object during its closest approach.

102
00:12:31,426 --> 00:12:32,855
Neither one heard anything.

103
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:38,112
A gentler version of the same silence idea came decades earlier.

104
00:12:38,112 --> 00:12:53,069
In nineteen seventy-three, Harvard radio astronomer John Ball proposed the Zoo Hypothesis: maybe advanced civilizations already know we're here, and are simply choosing not to interfere, the way a visitor at a nature reserve watches without stepping inside the fence.

105
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:57,190
It's a real, citable, seriously discussed idea.

106
00:12:57,190 --> 00:13:01,035
It's also, by its own logic, impossible to disprove.

107
00:13:01,035 --> 00:13:07,444
Any silence at all is consistent with a civilization that's simply choosing to watch quietly.

108
00:13:08,566 --> 00:13:19,843
Physicist Stephen Hawking made a similar argument in a very different context, back in twenty ten, warning that broadcasting our location to the universe could be like Native Americans first encountering Columbus.

109
00:13:19,843 --> 00:13:27,243
He never used the phrase "dark forest." The novel that coined it wouldn't be translated into English for another five years.

110
00:13:28,366 --> 00:13:32,292
That same caution shows up in a real, non-fictional policy fight.

111
00:13:32,292 --> 00:13:50,492
In the twenty-tens, several senior scientists linked to the search for alien signals resigned from committees to protest loosened rules on deliberately transmitting messages toward other stars, arguing that broadcasting our location, without asking the rest of the planet first, is a risk no single group has the right to take.

112
00:13:51,633 --> 00:13:54,559
Chapter eight. The signal that almost was.

113
00:13:55,700 --> 00:14:03,907
One hundred and twenty-four light-years away, there's a planet called K2-18b, bigger than Earth, smaller than Neptune, sitting in its star's habitable zone.

114
00:14:03,907 --> 00:14:12,471
For three straight years, it has been at the center of the closest real scientific fight over whether we've actually detected life anywhere else.

115
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:14,791
September twenty twenty-three.

116
00:14:14,791 --> 00:14:31,468
Cambridge astronomer Nikku Madhusudhan's team uses the James Webb Space Telescope and detects methane and carbon dioxide in its atmosphere, plus a faint, tentative hint of something else: dimethyl sulfide, a gas that, on Earth, is produced almost exclusively by marine microbes.

117
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:33,767
April twenty twenty-five.

118
00:14:33,767 --> 00:14:41,943
The same team publishes a new observation with a different instrument, reporting the signal again, this time at roughly three-sigma confidence.

119
00:14:41,943 --> 00:14:48,561
Cambridge's own press release calls it "the strongest hints yet of biological activity outside the solar system."

120
00:14:49,700 --> 00:14:51,113
Madhusudhan himself was careful.

121
00:14:51,113 --> 00:15:04,191
"It's important that we're deeply sceptical of our own results," he said, "because it's only by testing and testing again that we will be able to reach the point where we're confident in them." Three-sigma sounds impressive.

122
00:15:04,191 --> 00:15:07,019
A real discovery claim in physics needs five.

123
00:15:08,166 --> 00:15:13,215
Within weeks, NASA scientist Renyu Hu re-analyzed the same data with additional observations.

124
00:15:13,215 --> 00:15:25,642
He confirmed the methane and carbon dioxide again, but found the dimethyl sulfide signal only marginal, and identified ordinary, non-biological chemistry that could produce the same gas without any life at all.

125
00:15:26,766 --> 00:15:31,997
Two more independent teams ran the exact same raw data through their own statistical models.

126
00:15:31,997 --> 00:15:41,065
One found the signal supported in just twelve and a half percent of their tests, and traced most of it to unresolved instrument noise, not chemistry.

127
00:15:41,065 --> 00:15:46,645
Their conclusion, in their own words: "there is, as yet, no statistically significant evidence for biosignatures."

128
00:15:47,766 --> 00:15:53,625
By March twenty twenty-six, the newest paper on K2-18b doesn't even try to settle the biosignature question anymore.

129
00:15:53,625 --> 00:16:04,040
It argues the more basic physics of the planet, what its interior is even made of, has to be nailed down first, before anyone can evaluate a claim like this at all.

130
00:16:05,166 --> 00:16:20,433
So here is where it actually stands, right now: a real signal, detected twice, by the same team, using real telescope data, that has so far failed to survive three independent attempts to reproduce it.

131
00:16:20,433 --> 00:16:23,922
Not disproven. Not confirmed. Genuinely, actively, still open.

132
00:16:25,066 --> 00:16:28,148
Chapter nine. Back to the object that started this video.

133
00:16:29,266 --> 00:16:36,681
The interstellar object from our cold open passed its closest point to the sun at the end of October twenty twenty-five, and it has been fading ever since.

134
00:16:36,681 --> 00:16:41,183
By December it required a serious telescope just to see at all. It will never come back.

135
00:16:41,183 --> 00:16:48,597
It is now heading out of our solar system for good, on a path that will not bring it near the inner planets again for thousands of years.

136
00:16:49,733 --> 00:16:51,411
But the story isn't closed.

137
00:16:51,411 --> 00:17:07,522
New peer-reviewed papers on this object kept publishing well into twenty twenty-six, a methane detection in June, and a study months earlier, in May, of unusually heavy water that the researchers themselves say points to the object forming around a much older, colder star than our own sun.

138
00:17:08,666 --> 00:17:10,833
And Avi Loeb is still posting.

139
00:17:10,833 --> 00:17:14,444
His tally of "anomalies" kept growing well into twenty twenty-six.

140
00:17:14,444 --> 00:17:16,971
Jason Wright's rebuttals kept coming right back.

141
00:17:16,971 --> 00:17:20,943
As of this video, that argument has no scheduled end date.

142
00:17:22,066 --> 00:17:37,948
Astronomer Nathalie Cabrol, who leads the search for life at the Carl Sagan Center, put the bigger shift in perspective: "Planets became the rule, not the exception." Every year, the old excuse, that maybe habitable planets are just rare, gets a little harder to use.

143
00:17:39,066 --> 00:17:41,288
Here's what actually stays with me.

144
00:17:41,288 --> 00:17:49,436
Two real, live cases, one object, one planet, got about as close as anyone alive has ever gotten to answering this question.

145
00:17:49,436 --> 00:17:55,732
And both of them, honestly, ended in the exact same place: not yes, not no. Still open.

146
00:17:56,866 --> 00:17:58,261
That's not a failure.

147
00:17:58,261 --> 00:18:05,933
Seventy-five years ago, this entire argument existed because one physicist asked a question at lunch that nobody could even begin to test.

148
00:18:05,933 --> 00:18:12,559
Today, we have real telescopes pointed at real atmospheres, a hundred and twenty-four light-years away, arguing over real data.

149
00:18:12,559 --> 00:18:14,303
We haven't answered Fermi's question.

150
00:18:14,303 --> 00:18:17,790
We've finally earned the right to actually ask it properly.

151
00:18:18,933 --> 00:18:21,532
Fermi never got his answer. Neither have we.

152
00:18:21,532 --> 00:18:32,255
But for the first time since that lunch table in nineteen fifty, we're close enough to be genuinely, uncomfortably unsure, and that unsureness is the most honest place this question has ever been.

153
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:44,773
If two live cases like this got under your skin, wait until you see the message we already sent out into the dark, on purpose, in nineteen seventy-seven, and what would actually happen if anyone ever found it.

154
00:18:44,773 --> 00:18:47,767
That full story is live on this channel right now.

155
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:50,546
Subscribe to mindwired.

